<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tretyakov Gallery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as State Tretyakov Gallery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tretyakov Gallery is an art museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> holding the world's foremost collection of Russian art. It grew from the collection of the merchant Pavel Tretyakov, who began buying works in 1856 and gave the gallery to the city of Moscow in 1892. Its holdings include medieval icons such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-vladimir">Our Lady of Vladimir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-rublev">Andrei Rublev</a>'s Trinity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tretyakov-gallery.jpg" alt="Tretyakov Gallery" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Savin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moscow_05-2012_TretyakovGallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Muscovite merchant Pavel Mikhailovich Tretyakov began collecting art in the middle of 1850, and the gallery's founding year is taken to be 1856, when he bought two paintings by Russian artists of his day: Temptation by Nikolay Shilder and Skirmish with Finnish Smugglers by Vasily Khudyakov. Earlier, in 1854 and 1855, he had bought 11 drawings and nine pictures by Dutch Old Masters. His aim was a collection that might one day grow into a museum of national art. In 1867 the Moscow City Gallery of Pavel and Sergei Tretyakov was opened, holding 1,276 paintings, 471 sculptures and 10 drawings by Russian artists as well as 84 paintings by foreign masters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In August 1892 Tretyakov presented his collection to the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. It then included 1,287 paintings and 518 graphic works of the Russian school, 75 paintings and eight drawings of European schools, 15 sculptures and a collection of icons. The museum officially opened on 15 August 1893. It was housed in a mansion the Tretyakov family had bought in 1851 and extended several times. The façade, designed by the architect V. Bashkirov from drawings by the painter Viktor Vasnetsov in a Russian fairy-tale style, was built in 1902 to 1904.</p><p>On 3 June 1918 the gallery was declared the property of the Russian Federated Soviet Republic and named the State Tretyakov Gallery, with Igor Grabar as its director. In 1932 the former Church of St Nicholas in Tolmachi, closed in 1929, was given to the gallery. In the summer of 1941 the collection was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novosibirsk">Novosibirsk</a> on a train of 17 wagons, the paintings rolled on wooden shafts and packed in boxes, and the gallery reopened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 17 May 1945. The Alexander Ivanov Hall, built for his painting The Appearance of Christ Before the People, was completed in 1956. From 1986 to 1995 the main building in Lavrushinsky Lane was closed for a major renovation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The State Tretyakov Gallery is considered the foremost depository of Russian fine art in the world. Its collection runs to more than 130,000 exhibits, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-vladimir">Theotokos of Vladimir</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky">Wassily Kandinsky</a>'s Composition VII and Kazimir Malevich's Black Square. In 1977 it kept a significant part of the George Costakis collection. In 1985 it was merged with a gallery of contemporary art in the Central House of the Artists, whose grounds hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-realism">Socialist Realism</a> sculpture and statues of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> moved from their original sites. Its organisation also includes branches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Kaliningrad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tretyakov Gallery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The icon of Our Lady of Vladimir, painted in Constantinople in the early 12th century, was brought north by Andrei Bogolyubsky in 1155 and is now in Moscow’s <b>Tretyakov Gallery</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
